  "articlePanels": [
    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>An ISO 14001 certificate is not earned once. You keep proving the system is real. The internal audit is how you prove it to yourself first. Done well, the certification body confirms what you knew. Done as a tick-box the night before, the auditor finds the aspects register nobody touched in two years. Would your audit proof survive outside scrutiny?</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>ISO 14001:2015 needs an internal audit programme at planned intervals. The audit is itself audited.</li><li>Core areas: aspects and impacts, the legal register, objectives, and corrective actions.</li><li>Each nonconformity needs an action with an owner and a date. The next audit checks it closed.</li><li>The certification body runs surveillance audits between three-yearly recertifications. They sample your internal audit records.</li><li>Software does not certify you. Meeting the standard does. Quickler records the audit and keeps the proof.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Aspects and impacts",
      "heading": "Keeping the register live",
      "html": "<p>The heart of ISO 14001 is how your work meets the environment. Aspects are what you do, such as storing fuel or making waste. Impacts are the effects, such as contamination. You find them, judge which matter, and manage them. The audit checks the register is right for today, not for a process you stopped last year.</p><p>Quickler records your findings for each area, with photos where a site check backs up a document check. It does not keep your register for you. It records whether you found it current and complete.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Legal register",
      "heading": "Legal duties and duty of care",
      "html": "<p>ISO 14001 needs a register of legal and other requirements, and a regular check of compliance. On a UK site that means permit conditions, the waste duty of care in the Environmental Protection Act 1990, oil storage rules and discharge consents. A system that lists the law but never checks compliance has a gap the auditor will find.</p><p>Quickler lets you record, duty by duty, whether compliance was checked and proved. See the <a href=\"/pages/environmental-permit-compliance-audit-uk.html\">environmental permit compliance audit</a> guide for permits.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Findings and actions",
      "heading": "Nonconformities that get closed",
      "html": "<p>An audit is only worth it if findings lead to action. Grade each nonconformity. Find the root cause. Give it an owner and a date. Close it with proof. The next audit checks. The usual fault is an action raised, filed and forgotten. It comes back as a repeat finding and shakes the auditor's trust.</p><p>Quickler records each finding and its action. Open ones show on a dashboard. It does not close the action for you. It shows the overdue one to the person who can.</p>"
    },
